<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="AZD: Install and connect to Azure DevOps" id="Azd-Installation">

    <primary-label ref="azd"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>License: 10-day free trial, then <ui-path>Help | Register</ui-path></p>
        <p>Requires: a JetBrains IDE 2026.3, build 263.5153 or a later 2026.3 build</p>
        <p>Sign in: Microsoft, Azure CLI or personal access token (PAT), in an AZD tool window or <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path></p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Install AZD, start the trial, sign in to Azure DevOps Services or Azure DevOps Server, and renew expired tokens.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Install AZD and sign in with Microsoft, the Azure CLI or a personal access token.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Install the AZD plugin in JetBrains IDEs and connect it to Azure DevOps Services or Server with
        Microsoft sign-in, the Azure CLI or a personal access token.</web-summary>

    <p>
        Install AZD from JetBrains Marketplace, then connect it to your Azure DevOps organization.
        AZD stores one account per organization or collection, and every AZD tool window uses those accounts.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Before you start" id="azd-requirements">
        <list>
            <li>
                The current version requires a JetBrains IDE 2026.3, build 263.5153 or a later 2026.3 build.
                IDEs 2026.2 get AZD 2026.2.23, the final release for that IDE version.
            </li>
            <li>
                An account on Azure DevOps Services (<code>https://dev.azure.com/&lt;organization&gt;</code>)
                or on Azure DevOps Server 2020 or later.
            </li>
            <li>
                A Git repository with an Azure DevOps remote.
                The <control>AZD Pull Requests</control>, <control>AZD Pipelines</control> and
                <control>AZD Boards</control> tool windows appear only in projects that have such a remote.
            </li>
            <li>
                Optional: the Azure CLI (<code>az</code>) for <a anchor="azd-azcli-login">Azure CLI sign-in</a>,
                and Node.js 14 or later for the <a href="Azd-Pipeline-Yaml.topic">Azure Pipelines language server</a>.
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Install the plugin" id="azd_install">
        <tabs>
            <tab title="From the IDE" id="azd-from-ide-settings">
                <procedure title="Install AZD from the IDE" id="azd-installation-procedure">
                    <step>
                        <p>In the <control>Settings</control> dialog (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut>, macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>), select <control>Plugins</control>.</p>
                    </step>
                    <step>
                        <p>On the <control>Marketplace</control> tab, search for <code>AZD</code>.</p>
                        <!-- planned-image src="azd_install_marketplace.png" alt="Plugins settings with AZD in the Marketplace search results" -->
                    </step>
                    <step>
                        <p>Click <control>Install</control>, and restart the IDE if it asks you to.</p>
                    </step>
                </procedure>
            </tab>
            <tab title="From JetBrains Marketplace" id="azd-from-azd-marketplace-page">
                <procedure title="Install AZD from the Marketplace website" id="azd-install-from-web">
                    <step>
                        <p>Open the <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/22319-azd">AZD page on JetBrains Marketplace</a>.</p>
                    </step>
                    <step>
                        <p>Click the install button and select the running IDE.</p>
                    </step>
                </procedure>
            </tab>
        </tabs>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Start the trial or activate a license" id="azd-license">
        <p>
            AZD is a paid plugin with a 10-day free trial.
            Start the trial or activate a license in <ui-path>Help | Register</ui-path>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Buy or manage licenses on the <a href="https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/22319-azd/pricing">AZD pricing page</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Connect to Azure DevOps" id="connect">
        <p id="azd_connect">
            Sign in once per organization (Azure DevOps Services) or collection (Azure DevOps Server).
            You can sign in from any AZD tool window, from the <control>Clone Repository</control> dialog,
            or from <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path>.
        </p>

        <chapter title="Choose a sign-in method" id="azd_authentication">
            <table style="header-row">
                <tr>
                    <td>Method</td>
                    <td>Works with</td>
                    <td>Use it when</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><a anchor="azd-oauth-login">Sign in with Microsoft</a></td>
                    <td>Azure DevOps Services</td>
                    <td>You sign in with a Microsoft Entra ID account.
                        No token to create or renew.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><a anchor="azd-azcli-login">Sign in with Azure CLI</a></td>
                    <td>Azure DevOps Services</td>
                    <td>Your organization blocks OAuth apps or is phasing out personal access tokens, and you use <code>az login</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><a anchor="login-from-toolwindow">Personal access token</a></td>
                    <td>Azure DevOps Services and Azure DevOps Server</td>
                    <td>You connect to Azure DevOps Server, or you prefer a token with an expiry date.</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
            <p>
                Microsoft and Azure CLI sign-in sessions refresh on their own.
                If a sign-in error appears in a tool window, click <control>Log in again…</control>; see <a anchor="azd-renew-token"/>.
            </p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Sign in with Microsoft" id="azd-oauth-login">
            <p>
                The AZD tool windows appear only in a project with an Azure DevOps remote.
                Without one, sign in from <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path> (see below) or from the
                <a href="Azd-Manage-projects-hosted-on-Azure-DevOps.topic" anchor="clone-steps">Clone Repository dialog</a>.
            </p>
            <procedure title="Sign in with Microsoft from a tool window" id="azd-login-oauth-procedure">
                <step>
                    <p>Open the <control>AZD Pull Requests</control>, <control>AZD Pipelines</control> or <control>AZD Boards</control> tool window,
                        for example from <ui-path>View | Tool Windows</ui-path>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Check that the selector shows the right repository, and click <control>Sign in with Microsoft</control>.</p>
                    <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_toolwindow.png" alt="AZD Pull Requests tool window with the Sign in with Microsoft button and the Azure CLI and token links" -->
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In the <control>Sign in with Microsoft</control> dialog, choose the permissions to request, and click <control>Continue</control>:</p>
                    <list>
                        <li><control>Full access</control> (recommended): all features, including marking files as viewed in pull requests.</li>
                        <li><control>Standard access</control>: code, builds, pull requests and work items, but files cannot be marked as viewed.</li>
                    </list>
                    <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_oauth_scopes.png" alt="Sign in with Microsoft dialog with Full access selected" -->
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Sign in to Microsoft Entra ID in the browser that opens.</p>
                    <p>When the browser shows <control>Authentication Successful</control>, return to the IDE.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>
            <p>
                When AZD knows the organization, for example when you sign in from a tool window of a project with an Azure DevOps remote,
                it adds an account for that organization.
                Otherwise, for example from the <control>Clone Repository</control> dialog, it discovers every organization your account can reach
                and adds one account per organization.
                To sign in from Settings instead, click <control>+</control> on the accounts page and select
                <control>Sign in with Microsoft</control>.
            </p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Sign in with Azure CLI" id="azd-azcli-login">
            <p>AZD gets its token from <code>az account get-access-token</code>, so the Azure CLI must be installed and signed in.</p>
            <procedure title="Connect an Azure CLI account" id="azd-login-azcli-procedure">
                <step>
                    <p>In a terminal, run <code>az login</code>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In an AZD tool window, click <control>Sign in with Azure CLI</control>,
                        or in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path> click <control>+</control> and select <control>Sign in with Azure CLI</control>.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>
            <p>
                As with Microsoft sign-in, AZD adds an account for the organization of the repository.
                When it does not know the organization, for example in the <control>Clone Repository</control> dialog,
                it adds one account for each organization that your Azure CLI identity can reach.
            </p>
            <p>
                AZD looks for <code>az</code> on the <code>PATH</code> and in the usual install locations.
                If it cannot find it, set the path in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | Preferences</ui-path>,
                in the <control>Azure CLI</control> group, or click <control>Detect</control>.
            </p>
            <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_azcli_settings.png" alt="Azure CLI group on the AZD Preferences page with the detected az path" -->
            <p>
                If the Azure CLI session expires, AZD shows a notification.
                Run <code>az login</code> again, then retry.
            </p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Create a personal access token" id="generate_pat">
            <p id="azd_generate_pat">
                A personal access token (PAT) works with both Azure DevOps Services and Azure DevOps Server.
                Create it in the Azure DevOps web portal.
            </p>
            <procedure title="Create a PAT" id="azd-generate-pat-steps">
                <step>
                    <p>In Azure DevOps, open <ui-path>User settings | Personal access tokens</ui-path> and click <control>New Token</control>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Azure DevOps Services only: in <control>Organization</control>, select <control>All accessible organizations</control>.</p>
                    <img src="all_organization_pat.png" alt="Azure DevOps Create a new personal access token form with All accessible organizations selected"
                         width="636" border-effect="rounded"/>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Select <control>Custom defined</control> scopes and grant these scopes:</p>
                    <list columns="2" type="bullet">
                        <li><code>vso.build_execute</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.code_status</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.code_write</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.graph</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.identity</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.profile</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.project</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.test</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.threads_full</code></li>
                        <li><code>vso.work_write</code></li>
                    </list>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Click <control>Create</control> and copy the token.
                        Azure DevOps shows it only once.</p>
                    <p>Treat the token like a password.
                        Do not share it or commit it to source control.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>

            <note title="Mark as viewed needs a Full access token">
                <p>
                    Azure DevOps lets only a <control>Full access</control> token mark pull request files as viewed.
                    The scopes above cover every other AZD feature.
                </p>
            </note>

            <chapter title="What each scope is for" id="azd-scope-details" collapsible="true">
                <table style="header-row">
                    <tr>
                        <td>Scope</td>
                        <td>Name in the Azure DevOps portal</td>
                        <td>Used for</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.code_write</code></td><td>Code (Read &amp; write)</td><td>Cloning, pull requests and pushing changes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.code_status</code></td><td>Code (Status)</td><td>Pull request and build statuses</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.graph</code></td><td>Graph (Read)</td><td>Finding users and groups</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.build_execute</code></td><td>Build (Read &amp; execute)</td><td>Running and watching pipelines</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.identity</code></td><td>Identity (Read)</td><td>Reading user identities</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.project</code></td><td>Project and Team (Read)</td><td>Reading projects and teams</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.threads_full</code></td><td>Pull Request Threads (Read &amp; write)</td><td>Pull request comments</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.profile</code></td><td>User Profile (Read)</td><td>Your profile and avatar</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.work_write</code></td><td>Work Items (Read &amp; write)</td><td>Creating, editing and linking work items</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>vso.test</code></td><td>Test Management (Read)</td><td>Test results and test cases</td></tr>
                </table>
            </chapter>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Sign in with a personal access token" id="login-from-toolwindow">
            <p id="azd-pat-login-service">
                Enter the server URL and the organization or collection in separate fields.
                Do not add the organization or collection to the URL.
            </p>
            <procedure title="Sign in with a PAT from a tool window" id="azd-login-pat-toolwindow">
                <step>
                    <p>In an AZD tool window, click <control>Use token…</control>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In <control>Server</control>, enter <code>https://dev.azure.com</code>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Paste the token in <control>Token</control>.
                        <control>Generate…</control> opens Microsoft's instructions for creating a token.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In <control>Organization/Collection</control>, enter the organization name, for example <code>demo-org</code>.</p>
                    <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_pat_dialog.png" alt="Add AZD Account dialog with the server URL and organization filled in for Azure DevOps Services" -->
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Click <control>Log In</control>.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>
            <procedure title="Add a PAT account in Settings" id="azd-login-pat-procedure-service">
                <step>
                    <p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Click <control>+</control> and select <control>Sign in with Personal Access Token</control>.</p>
                    <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_add_account_popup.png" alt="AZD accounts page with the add account menu open" -->
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In the <control>Add AZD Account</control> dialog, fill in <control>Server</control>, <control>Token</control>
                        and <control>Organization/Collection</control>, and click <control>Log In</control>.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>
            <p>
                If AZD reports missing scopes, create a new token with the <a anchor="generate_pat">scopes listed above</a>
                for <control>All accessible organizations</control>.
            </p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Connect to Azure DevOps Server" id="azd-azure-devops-server">
            <p>
                Azure DevOps Server supports only personal access tokens.
                Microsoft and Azure CLI sign-in work only with Azure DevOps Services.
            </p>
            <procedure title="Sign in to Azure DevOps Server" id="azd-login-pat-procedure-server">
                <step>
                    <p>On your server, open <ui-path>User settings | Personal access tokens</ui-path> and create a token with the <a anchor="azd-scope-details">scopes listed above</a>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In an AZD tool window, click <control>Use token…</control>, or add an account in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In <control>Server</control>, enter the server URL without the collection, for example <code>https://ado.example.com</code>.
                        If the server runs under a <code>tfs</code>, <code>azd</code>, <code>devops</code> or <code>azure</code> virtual directory,
                        include it, for example <code>https://ado.example.com/tfs</code>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Paste the token in <control>Token</control>.</p>
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>In <control>Organization/Collection</control>, enter the collection name, for example <code>DefaultCollection</code>.</p>
                    <!-- planned-image src="azd_signin_server_dialog.png" alt="Add AZD Account dialog with an Azure DevOps Server URL and the DefaultCollection collection" -->
                </step>
                <step>
                    <p>Click <control>Log In</control>.</p>
                </step>
            </procedure>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Manage accounts" id="azd-manage-accounts">
        <p>All accounts are listed in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path>.</p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_settings_accounts.png" alt="AZD accounts page with one account set as default and Clone git repositories using ssh selected" -->
        <list>
            <li>To add an account, click <control>+</control> and choose a sign-in method.</li>
            <li>To remove an account, select it and click <control>−</control>.</li>
            <li>To choose the account that AZD uses by default, select it and click <control>Set as Default</control>.</li>
            <li>To renew an expired or revoked token, see <a anchor="azd-renew-token"/>.</li>
            <li>To switch the repository or account that the AZD tool windows use, see <a anchor="azd-select-repository"/>.</li>
        </list>
        <p>
            Each account shows its sign-in method after its name: <control>[OAuth]</control> for Microsoft sign-in,
            <control>[Azure CLI]</control> or <control>[PAT]</control>.
        </p>
        <p>
            AZD stores credentials in the IDE password storage.
            Accounts are not synchronized by Backup and Sync, so sign in again on each computer.
            If the IDE stores passwords in memory only, the accounts page shows a warning; see
            <a href="Azd-FAQ.topic" anchor="token_issue">Credentials are not saved</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Renew a token" id="azd-renew-token">
        <p>
            When a token expires or is revoked, the AZD tool windows show a sign-in error,
            and the account in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path> shows <control>Invalid access token</control>.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Renew the token of an account" id="azd-renew-token-procedure">
            <step>
                <p>If the account uses a personal access token, create a new token; see <a anchor="generate_pat"/>.
                    If it uses the Azure CLI, run <code>az login</code> in a terminal.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>In the AZD tool window, click <control>Log in again…</control> in the error message,
                    or click <control>Log in</control> next to the account in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD</ui-path>.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>For a personal access token, paste the new token in the <control>Update AZD Account</control> dialog and click <control>Log In</control>.
                    For Microsoft sign-in, the <control>Sign in with Microsoft</control> dialog opens; see <a anchor="azd-oauth-login"/>.
                    For the Azure CLI, AZD gets a new token from <code>az</code>.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Open the AZD tool windows" id="azd-tool-windows">
        <p>
            AZD adds three tool windows to projects that have an Azure DevOps Git remote:
            <control>AZD Pull Requests</control>, <control>AZD Pipelines</control> and <control>AZD Boards</control>.
            Open them from <ui-path>View | Tool Windows</ui-path> or from the tool window bar.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can also select <ui-path>Git | AZD | Show AZD Pull Requests</ui-path>.
        </p>
        <list>
            <li><a href="Work-with-Azure-DevOps-pull-requests.topic">Work with pull requests</a></li>
            <li><a href="Azure-DevOps-Pipelines.topic">Work with pipelines</a></li>
            <li><a href="Azd-Boards.topic">Boards and work items</a></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Choose the repository and account" id="azd-select-repository">
        <p>
            The <control>AZD Pull Requests</control>, <control>AZD Pipelines</control> and <control>AZD Boards</control> tool windows
            share one connection: one repository and one account.
            AZD connects on its own when the project has one Azure DevOps repository and you have a matching account,
            or when you connected before.
            Otherwise, the tool window shows a repository selector and an account selector.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Connect the AZD tool windows to a repository" id="azd-select-repository-procedure">
            <step>
                <p>Open an AZD tool window, for example <ui-path>View | Tool Windows | AZD Pipelines</ui-path>.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Select the repository in the repository selector.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click the account selector and select an account.
                    The same list offers the sign-in methods, and <control>Renew Token</control> for the selected account.</p>
                <p>If you have no account for the organization or server of the repository, sign in with the buttons in the tool window;
                    see <a anchor="azd_authentication"/>.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Click <control>View Pull Requests</control>, <control>View Pipelines</control> or <control>View Work Items</control>,
                    depending on the tool window.</p>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            All three tool windows then show the selected repository.
            AZD remembers the choice and reconnects the next time you open the project.
        </p>
        <p>
            To switch later, click <control>⋮</control> in the <control>AZD Pull Requests</control> tool window header
            and select <control>Change Repository or Account…</control>.
            This action is available only when the project has more than one Azure DevOps remote.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Open the welcome guide" id="azd-welcome-guide">
        <p>
            Select <ui-path>Help | Show Welcome Guide</ui-path> to open the <control>AZD Welcome</control> tool window.
            It walks you through six steps: an overview, connecting to Azure DevOps, pull requests, pipelines, AI features, and resources.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_welcome_guide.png" alt="AZD Welcome tool window showing the first step of the guide" -->
    </chapter>

